  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how diabetes mellitus contributes to bone disease and how current osteoporosis therapies may counter it. At the top, diabetes is shown driving three linked problems in bone: increased resorption, impaired formation, and microarchitectural deterioration. These pathways converge on overall bone loss, which then leads toward the goal of bone restoration if effectively treated.\n\nThe left side groups antiresorptive drugs, mainly bisphosphonates and denosumab. Their arrows indicate suppression of bone resorption and a resulting reduction in bone loss. The right side groups anabolic agents, including romosozumab and parathyroid hormone or abaloparatide. These treatments appear to stimulate bone formation and also influence structural deterioration, suggesting a broader rebuilding effect on weakened bone.\n\nThe central layout emphasizes that diabetic bone disease is not caused by a single defect but by an imbalance between breakdown and rebuilding, along with damage to bone quality or internal structure. The drug classes are positioned as acting on different parts of this network: antiresorptives mainly block excess breakdown, whereas anabolic therapies promote formation and may help restore architecture. Overall, the image conveys a treatment framework for diabetes-related bone fragility based on targeting multiple mechanisms.",
